course: dictionary
lesson: 12947
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the author means. Exceptional has two main meanings. The first is extremely good: «Maya's painting was exceptional, far better than anything else in the class.» The clue is far better than anything else, so exceptional means very, very good.
explain: The second meaning is unusual and rare: «It was exceptional to see snow in May.» Here it does not praise anything. It tells you that snow in May does not happen often. Ask yourself: is the sentence praising something, or saying something is rare? That question tells you which meaning to use.
explain: In rules, exceptional often means only in rare cases: «Children may go home early only in exceptional circumstances.» Swap in your meaning to check it still makes sense: only in rare cases makes sense here, so you have it right.
fact: Exceptional means extremely good, much better than usual.
fact: Exceptional also means unusual and not likely to happen often.
fact: «An exceptional talent» means a rare gift that most people do not have.
fact: «In exceptional circumstances» means only in rare cases.
fact: Exceptionally is the adverb form, as in «She was exceptionally quick.»
exercise: «The young violinist played with exceptional skill.» What does exceptional mean here? | Very cold | *Extremely good, much better than usual | Ordinary and plain | Late for the concert
exercise: «Snow in the desert is exceptional; it happens only once in many years.» What does exceptional mean here? | *Unusual and rare | Loud and noisy | Wet and muddy | Extremely good
exercise: Find the adverb in this sentence: «She sang exceptionally well at the school show.» | sang | *exceptionally | school | show
test: «Her exceptional memory helped her win the quiz.» What does exceptional suggest about her memory? | Ordinary, like everyone else's | Bad and forgetful | Lost somewhere | *Extremely good, much better than most people's
test: Mum says, «Staying home from school is allowed only in exceptional circumstances.» What does this mean? | Whenever the children ask | On Fridays only | *Only in rare cases | Every single day
exam: Which use of exceptional means unusual and rare, not praise? | The exceptional chef cooked dinner | He is an exceptional runner | Her exceptional painting won the prize | *Snow in May is exceptional in our town
